  "title": "Origins of religion and ritual burial",
  "summary": "At Qafzeh Cave in Israel, about 100,000 years ago, modern humans intentionally buried as many as 15 individuals, with 71 pieces of red ochre near the bones, which suggests ritual. Burial rituals may imply a belief that a person's identity extends beyond death. Shell beads from such sites also point to symbolic behaviour. By about 9,600 BCE, hunter-gatherers at Göbekli Tepe in Anatolia raised megalithic monuments probably used in rituals, with pillars carved with wild animals.",
